5:52:21 AM [express] GET /api/records/user/1 304 in 4ms :: [{"id":2,"userId":1,"symptom":"i am having high fever","diagnosis":"1.  What could this be?\n    *   Most Common: Viral infection such as influenza (flu) or mononucleosis (\"mono\"). Bacterial infections less likely given lack of localized findings. Other possibilities include malaria depending on location.\n    *   Less Common: Less likely bacterial infections without focal signs; autoimmune disorders presenting atypically; drug reaction.\n\n2.  To which doctor should I refer?\n    *   General Physician: The initial point of contact for assessing overall health status and directing further management. They can evaluate the presentation and determine appropriate next steps.\n    *   Infectious Disease Specialist: Could become necessary if viral cause cannot be identified quickly after initial evaluation by GP.\n    *   Rheumatology: Consider referral if there is suspicion of inflammatory arthritis causing muscle aches alongside fever.\n\n3.  Should I be worried?\n    *   MAYBE - High fever warrants investigation, especially when accompanied by sweating and muscle aches lasting over a week. It needs proper diagnosis via examination & testing to rule out serious underlying causes.\n\n4.  Do I need to go to a hospital right now?\n    *   Go to Hospital Immediately IF experiencing: Severe headache, stiff neck, confusion, difficulty breathing, chest pain, persistent vomiting, seizures, rash spreading rapidly, extreme lethargy/unconsciousness. These require emergent intervention. Otherwise, schedule an appointment with a general physician.\n\n5.  How urgent is this?\n    *   See a Doctor Today – Given the persistence of the fever and accompanying symptoms despite being present for a week, prompt medical attention is recommended. Delaying treatment could potentially lead to complications.\n\n6.  What can I do right now at home?\n    *   Rest completely. Stay hydrated by drinking plenty of fluids (water, electrolyte solutions). Take acetaminophen (paracetamol) according to package directions for fever relief. Avoid strenuous activity until recovered. Monitor temperature regularly.\n\n7.  What should I avoid?\n    *   Avoid alcohol consumption while unwell. Avoid exposure to others who may also be ill. Avoid heavy meals initially, focus on easily digestible food once appetite returns.\n\n8.  What should I eat or drink?\n    *   Eat light, nutritious foods easy to digest, such as broth-based soups, plain toast, bananas, rice, applesauce. Continue hydrating well throughout the day.\n\n9.  Can I take any medicine on my own?\n    *   Acetaminophen (Paracetamol): Safe dose typically up to 500mg every 4–6 hours as needed for fever and mild discomfort. Ibuprofen can be considered too, however use cautiously due to potential gastrointestinal side effects. Aspirin should generally be avoided in children and adolescents because of increased risk of Reye’s syndrome.\n    *   **Important Disclaimer:** *Do not self-medicate. Please consult a General Physician before taking any medication.*\n\n10. What symptoms should I watch out for?\n    *   Worsening cough, shortness of breath, rapid heart rate, dizziness, decreased urine output, inability to keep down liquids, skin turning blueish discoloration around lips or fingers. Any sudden change in neurological function requires immediate medical attention.\n\n11. Is this related to something I already have?\n    *   The provided information does not contain enough details regarding past illnesses, vaccinations, or known allergies to definitively link the symptoms directly back to pre-existing conditions. However, consider whether previous bouts of flu or similar respiratory illness occurred recently. Also assess vaccination status against preventable diseases.\n\n12. What will the doctor likely ask me?\n    *   Duration of fever, its highest recorded value, description of sweat production during fever episodes, characterization of muscle aches (location, intensity), presence of chills, any preceding cold symptoms, possible exposures to individuals with infectious disease, existing medical problems, medications currently taken, allergies, vaccination records, and travel history.\n\n13. What tests might the doctor suggest?\n    *   Complete blood count (CBC) to check white blood cell levels indicating infection. Rapid strep test or throat swab culture to exclude streptococcal pharyngitis . Urinalysis to assess kidney function and look for urinary tract infection. Chest X-ray to investigate lung involvement. Depending upon clinical picture, PCR testing for various viruses including Influenza virus, RSV, Adenovirus, EBV, CMV, HIV, Hepatitis B&C serologies, Malaria smear microscopy.","fullConversation":{"type":"symptom_check","onset":"last week","severity":"severe","selectedRelated":["Sweating","Muscle Aches"]},"createdAt":"2026-03-19T05:13:12.517Z"},{"id":1,"userId":1,"symptom":"i had fever","diagnosis":"1.  What could this be?\n    Possible causes for prolonged high fever (>14 days) with sweating include infection (bacterial, viral, fungal), inflammatory conditions, autoimmune disorders, drug reactions, heatstroke, or even certain cancers though less likely given age group. The presence of sweating suggests either hyperthermia due to infection or inflammation, or possibly anxiety depending on context.\n\n2.  To which doctor should I refer?\n    Start by referring to a General Practitioner (GP)/Family Doctor. They can assess the overall clinical picture, perform initial examinations, consider possible diagnoses, and then refer appropriately if needed. Further referrals would depend on the GP’s findings; possibilities include infectious disease specialist, rheumatology consultant, dermatologist, oncologist, or neurologist, among others.\n\n3.  Should I be worried?\n    YES - Prolonged fever warrants investigation because it indicates underlying pathology potentially requiring treatment. It also raises concern for serious infections such as typhoid, malaria, dengue, or sepsis.\n\n4.  Do I need to go to a hospital right now?\n    Go to the Emergency Department immediately if experiencing: High grade fever accompanied by stiff neck, altered mental status, seizures, difficulty breathing, chest pain, abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, inability to keep fluids down, significant dehydration (decreased urination, dizziness upon standing), or rapid heart rate. Otherwise, schedule an appointment with a general practitioner soonest.\n\n5.  How urgent is this?\n    See a doctor today – Given the persistence of the fever exceeding two weeks, prompt evaluation is necessary to rule out treatable etiologies. Delaying diagnosis could lead to complications.\n\n6.  What can I do right now at home?\n    Rest completely. Stay hydrated by drinking plenty of water, electrolyte solutions, broth, or diluted juice. Avoid strenuous activity. Monitor temperature regularly. Consider over-the-counter acetaminophen or ibuprofen for fever relief *if* no contraindications exist (like kidney problems or bleeding disorder). Apply cool compresses to forehead and armpits.\n\n7.  What should I avoid?\n    Avoid alcohol consumption until diagnosed. Limit caffeine intake, especially if dehydrated. Avoid spicy or greasy food while ill. Avoid exposure to crowds during acute illness phase.\n\n8.  What should I eat or drink?\n    Eat easily digestible bland foods like soup, rice, toast, bananas, applesauce, and plain yogurt. Drink frequent small sips of liquids throughout the day to prevent dehydration. Ensure adequate sodium replacement through oral rehydration salts if tolerated.\n\n9.  Can I take any medicine on my own?\n    Acetaminophen (Tylenol): Adults & children > 1 year old: Take up to 1000mg every 4–6 hours as directed on package label OR Ibuprofen (Advil, Motrin): Adults & Children > 6 months :Take up to 400 mg every 4–6 hours as directed on package label. **Important:** DO NOT exceed recommended dosages. Stop using and seek medical advice IMMEDIATELY IF ANY SIDE EFFECTS occur (rash, itching, swelling, shortness of breath, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea). Individuals with liver or kidney problems, ulcers, asthma, or who are pregnant or breastfeeding must CONSULT A PHYSICIAN BEFORE USING THESE MEDICATIONS.\n\n10. What symptoms should I watch out for?\n    Watch closely for worsening headache, confusion, lethargy, muscle weakness, skin rash, jaundice(yellowing of eyes or skin), decreased urine output, increased respiratory rate, or changes in bowel habits. Seek IMMEDIATE medical attention if any of these develop.\n\n11. Is this related to something I already have?\nThe patient's young age makes some chronic illnesses less likely primary drivers of this presentation however his blood group being O+ does raise suspicion regarding potential hemolytic anemia although rare without further information its difficult to determine whether he has pre existing health concerns contributing to the fever . His height and weight are normal ranges therefore unlikely to cause secondary effects directly impacting the immune system unless there are additional factors involved. There isn't enough detail provided here to definitively link the symptoms solely to known past conditions. However, knowing details about prior surgeries, vaccinations, previous illnesses, and any ongoing treatments would help clarify associations between them and present complaints.\n\n12. What will the doctor likely ask me?\nThe doctor will inquire about onset date, duration, highest recorded temperature, alleviating or aggravating factors, associated symptoms (headache, body aches, cough, sore throat, fatigue, loss of appetite, gastrointestinal upset), travel history, occupation, social contacts, vaccination records, any known allergies, current medications, medical history including previous fevers, immunizations, surgical procedures, and family history of similar ailments.\n\n13. What tests might the doctor suggest?\nCommonly ordered tests include complete blood count (CBC) with differential, erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) or C-reactive protein (CRP) levels, urinalysis, stool studies (for bacterial pathogens), blood cultures, serological testing for various infections (malaria, Dengue, Typhoid, HIV), imaging studies (chest X-ray, CT scan abdomen/pelvis) depending on suspected etiology, and possibly specialized tests tailored to the individual case after preliminary results come back.","fullConversation":{"type":"symptom_check","onset":"more than 14 days","severity":"severe","selectedRelated":["Sweating"]},"createdAt":"2026-03-17T18:11:10.990Z"}]
