5:56:30 AM [express] POST /api/ai/chat 200 in 92694ms :: {"response":"Okay, here’s a tailored diet plan for Mani considering his current lab results. I will focus on addressing the low sodium and potassium while being mindful of the mildly high creatinine level. Remember, consistency is key!\n\n## 🌅 Breakfast\n*   **Idli Sambar:** 2 Idlis (approx. 150 kcal, 5g protein, 30g carbs, 5g fat) - *Provides carbohydrates for energy.*\n*   **Small Glass of Buttermilk (Chaas):** 1 cup (approx. 60 kcal, 2g protein, 10g carbs, 0g fat) – *Helps replenish electrolytes like sodium.*\n\n## 🍎 Mid-Morning Snack\n*   **Banana:** 1 medium banana (approx. 105 kcal, 1.3 g protein, 27g carbs, 0.4g fat) – *Good source of potassium.*\n*   **Dry Fruits Mix**: 1 tbsp mixed dry fruits(almonds, cashews, raisins)(approx. 70kcal, 2g protien , 15g carb, 5g fat)- *provides some micronutrients and fiber*.\n\n## 🍽️ Lunch\n*   **Dal Makhani (small portion):** 1 bowl (approx. 300 kcal, 15g protein, 40g carbs, 15g fat) – *Complex carbohydrate from lentils helps regulate glucose levels.*\n*   **Vegetable Rice:** ½ cup cooked vegetable rice (approx. 150 kcal, 3g protein, 30g carbs, 1g fat) – *Low Glycemic Index Carbohydrate Source.*\n*    **Cucumber Raita:** 1/4 cup cucumber raita (approx. 50 kcal, 1g protein, 5g carbs, 2g fat) -- *Hydrating and provides some minerals.*\n\n## 🌆 Evening Snack\n*   **Sweet Potato Fries:** 1 cup baked sweet potato fries (approx. 150 kcal, 2g protein, 30g carbs, 1g fat) – *Offers complex carbohydrates and vitamin A.*\n*  **Coconut Water:** 1 glass (approx. 45 kcal, 0g protein, 10g carbs, 0g fat) – *Replenishes lost electrolytes especially potassium.*\n\n## 🌙 Dinner\n*   **Egg White Curry:** 2 Egg Whites stir fry with spices (approx. 150 kcal, 10g protein, 5g carbs, 5g fat) – *Lean protein option.*\n*   **Roti:** 2 whole wheat rotis (approx. 200 kcal, 8g protein, 40g carbs, 2g fat) – *Carbohydrates for sustained energy.*\n*   **Spinach Salad:** Small serving of Spinach salad with lemon juice dressing (approx. 50 kcal, 2g protein, 5g carbs, 1g fat) – *Rich in vitamins and minerals.*\n\n## ⚠️ Foods to Avoid\n*   Excessive Salted Snacks\n*   Processed Meats\n*   High Sugar Drinks\n*   Foods High in Purine content such as Red Meat, Shellfish etc.(Unless specifically advised otherwise.)\n\n## 💡 Why This Plan\nThis plan aims to address Mani's low sodium and potassium levels through strategic inclusion of foods rich in these elements. The moderately increased protein intake supports overall health without exacerbating potential kidney strain given the mild elevation in creatinine. Hydration throughout the day is crucial due to the slightly elevated creatinine. It also includes lower GI options which can assist with better control of glucose levels if there were concerns about diabetes risk factors present elsewhere in the clinical picture.","followUpQuestions":[]}
