6:12:08 AM [express] POST /api/ai/chat 200 in 82882ms :: {"response":"Okay, here's a dietary plan tailored specifically for Mani based on his profile and lab results as provided. I will prioritize addressing the low sodium and low potassium levels while being mindful of the slightly elevated creatinine. Remember, this is general advice; consulting directly with a registered dietitian or physician specializing in renal health would be ideal before making significant changes to his diet.\n\n**Dietary Plan for Mani**\n\n**Breakfast:**\n\n*   Oatmeal made with milk (dairy OR fortified plant-based alternative), topped with berries (strawberries, blueberries - good source of antioxidants which can help protect kidneys!), nuts (almonds, walnuts – healthy fats & some minerals), and a sprinkle of cinnamon. A small serving of scrambled eggs cooked without added salt if he prefers them.\n    *   Rationale: Provides carbohydrates from oatmeal, fiber from fruit and nuts, and protein from eggs. The choice of dairy or plant-based milk should consider any lactose intolerance or allergies. Cinnamon has potential benefits related to blood sugar control but more research needed regarding kidney function.\n\n**Mid-Morning Snack:**\n\n*   Small handful of mixed unsalted nuts and seeds (pumpkin seeds, sunflower seeds, chia seeds). Alternatively, plain yogurt (Greek Yogurt preferred due to higher protein content) with a few berries.\n    *   Rationale: Offers essential fatty acids, vitamins, and minerals through the nuts and seeds. Plain yogurt provides calcium and probiotics. Unsalted versions prevent excess sodium intake.\n\n**Lunch:**\n\n*   Large salad composed primarily of leafy greens (spinach, romaine lettuce), cucumbers, tomatoes, bell peppers, carrots, and avocado. Grilled chicken breast (or tofu for vegetarian option) seasoned lightly with herbs like basil, oregano, rosemary, and garlic powder instead of salt. Olive oil and vinegar dressing.\n    *   Rationale: High volume of vegetables provide hydration and nutrients. Lean protein helps maintain muscle mass. Using fresh herbs adds flavor without adding sodium. Avocado offers healthy monounsaturated fat. Limit high oxalate veggies such as spinach until further evaluation done because they may cause issues later down the road.\n\n**Evening Snack:**\n\n*   Banana with a tablespoon of almond butter. Or Coconut Water.\n    *   Rationale: Bananas offer potassium along with other electrolytes. Almond butter contributes healthy fats and protein. Coconut water is hydrating and contains electrolytes.\n\n**Dinner:**\n\n*   Baked salmon fillet served alongside roasted asparagus and quinoa. Seasoning with lemon juice, pepper, and dill. Small portion of steamed green beans.\n    *   Rationale: Salmon provides omega-3 fatty acids beneficial for overall health. Asparagus is relatively low in potassium compared to many other vegetables. Quinoa is a complete protein and easy to digest. Lemon juice enhances flavour over using salt. Green Beans have lower potassium than most vegetables listed earlier.\n\n**Foods To Avoid / Restrict Significantly:**\n\n*   Processed meats (bacon, sausage, deli meat)\n*   Fast food restaurants\n*   Canned soups containing large amounts of sodium\n*   Salty snacks (chips, pretzels, crackers)\n*   Excessive use of table salt during cooking or at the dinner table\n*   High-sodium condiments (soy sauce, ketchup, mustard)\n*   Alcoholic beverages (especially beer)\n*   Certain fruits and vegetables known to be high in potassium (see note below about spinach). *This list isn’t exhaustive.* Always consult with a healthcare professional for specific guidance.\n\n**Why This Plan?**\n\n*   **Low Sodium:** The primary goal is to manage the low sodium level. The plan emphasizes avoiding processed foods, limiting salty seasonings, and focusing on naturally occurring sources of sodium found in whole foods rather than relying heavily on table salt. We avoid restricting sodium completely since his current reading is not critically low. If readings continue to drop we need to reevaluate.\n*   **Low Potassium:** Addressing the low potassium involves incorporating potassium-rich","followUpQuestions":[]}
