6:25:45 AM [express] POST /api/ai/chat 200 in 97949ms :: {"response":"Okay, here's a muscle building diet plan designed for Mani, considering his parameters. Note that optimal macros and timing might require adjustments depending on individual responses and workout intensity. Consulting with a qualified sports nutrition specialist is always recommended for precise tailoring.\n\n**Important Considerations Before Starting:**\n\n*   **Training Schedule:** This plan assumes regular resistance training sessions (e.g., 3-5 times per week). Adjust carbohydrate intake around intense lifting days.\n*   **Individual Needs:** Macronutrient ratios (protein, carbs, fat) often range between 1.6-2.2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight. Carbohydrate intake depends largely on training frequency and intensity.\n*   **Listen to Your Body:** Pay attention to energy levels, recovery time, and adjust accordingly.\n*   **Supplements:** While supplements aren't mandatory, creatine monohydrate is generally considered safe and effective for enhancing performance and promoting muscle growth. Discuss appropriate dosages with a healthcare provider.\n\n**Diet Plan for Muscle Gain (Mani)**\n\n**Assumptions:**\n\n*   Weight = 77 kg\n*   Height= 5.11 ft\n\nBased off those assumptions, a reasonable macronutrient split could look something like this:\n\n*   Protein: ~190 g (around 2.2g/kg)\n*   Carbohydrates: ~350 g\n*   Fats: ~70 g\n\nThese numbers are estimates and must be adjusted according to personal preferences and tolerance.\n\n**Breakfast (approx. 500-600 kcal):**\n\n*   Whey Protein Shake (30g whey isolate) blended with Banana (medium size) and Oats (1/2 cup dry). Consider adding a scoop of casein protein post workout too.\n    *   Rationale: Whey protein delivers fast-digesting amino acid support crucial for initiating muscle repair and growth following morning workouts. Complex carbohydrates from bananas replenish glycogen stores depleted overnight. Oats contribute sustained energy release. Casein can also aid in slow digestion overnight.\n\n**Mid-Morning Snack (approx. 300-400 kcal):**\n\n*   Chicken Breast (grilled or poached, approx. 150g) with Brown Rice Cakes (2 medium sized) and Avocado Slices (1/4).\n    *   Rationale: Lean protein supports continued muscle synthesis. Brown rice provides easily digestible carbohydrates. Healthy fats from avocado promote hormone production necessary for muscle development.\n\n**Lunch (approx. 600-700 kcal):**\n\n*   Lean Ground Beef (150g) stir fry with Mixed Veggies (Broccoli, Peppers, Mushrooms, Spinach) and Quinoa (1 cup cooked). Soy Sauce used sparingly.\n    *   Rationale: High quality protein promotes muscle growth. Variety of colorful vegetables ensures micronutrient intake. Quinoa serves as a complex carb providing sustained energy.\n\n**Pre-Workout Snack (approx. 200-300kcal):**\n\n*   Fruit Smoothie consisting of Berries (mixed types), Milk Alternative (Almond or Skimmed Cow's Milk) and Peanut Butter (1 tbsp).\n     * Rationale: Quick digesting carbohydrates and protein boost energy prior to working out.\n\n**Post-Workout Recovery (within 30 minutes) (approx. 300-400 kcal):**\n\n*   Casein Protein shake (30g) with water or milk alternative.\n    *   Rationale: Slow releasing protein aids in repairing damaged tissues and replenishing nutrient stores.\n\n**Dinner (approx. 600-700 kcal):**\n\n*   Salmon Steak (150g) baked or grilled with Sweet Potatoes (one medium sized) and Steamed Broccoli. Drizzle with olive oil spray.\n    *   Rationale: Excellent source of Omega","followUpQuestions":[]}
