on Results Unit Bio. Ref. Inter-\nval\nMethod\nCalcium, Serum\nCalcium Total 6.4 mg/dl 8.4 - 10.2 Enzymatic colorimetric\nInterpretation\nIncreased Calcium (Hypercalcemia) Decreased Calcium (Hypocalcemia)\n• Primary hyperparathyroidism\n• Malignancy (PTHrP-producing tumors, bone metastases)\n• Vitamin D intoxication\n• Granulomatous diseases (sarcoidosis, TB)\n• Thiazide diuretics\n• Immobilization\n• Hypoparathyroidism\n• Vitamin D deficiency\n• Chronic kidney disease (secondary hyperparathyroidism)\n• Hypoalbuminemia (low total calcium, ionized may be normal)\n• Acute pancreatitis\n• Magnesium deficiency\n• Massive transfusions (citrate binding)\nAssociated Tests:\nSerum Albumin, Ionized Calcium, Phosphate, Parathyroid Hormone (PTH), Vitamin D (25-OH, 1,25-OH‚ D), Magnesium, Renal function tests (Urea,\nCreatinine, eGFR).\nReference :\n• The Harriet Lane Handbook, 23rd Edition Page no. 737\n• Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22nd Edition, Page no. 396\n• Kit Insert\n----- End of Report -----\nPatient Registered on :09-03-2026, 11:12 AM\nSample Collected on : 09-03-2026, 11:12 AM\nTest Released on : 09-03-2026, 11:20 AM\nSample ID : 69AE5DDD0EAFC\nSample Type : Serum Dr. Hemawati Dhritlahare\nMD (Medicine, Gold Medalist), PhD (Cardiology)\nPage : 3 / 5 Powered By :\n\n-- 3 of 5 --\n\nPatient ID :\nPatient Name : Mr. Ravi Kumar\nAge / Gender : 55 Years / Male\nOrder Status : Final\nRef. By : Self\nReport Date : 09-03-2026\nSample Collected at : Pt. J.N.M Medical college\nMedical College, Raipur, Raipur, Chhattisgarh, 490001\nSample Processed at : Dhritlahare Diagnostics & Pathology\n, Raipur, Raipur, Chhattisgarh, 492001\nClinical Chemistry\nInvestigation Results Unit Bio. Ref. Inter-\nval\nMethod\nPhosphorus, Serum\nPhosphorus 5 mg/dL 2.8 - 4.5 UV Molybdate colorimetric\nHigh Phosphorus Levels (Hyperphosphatemia)\n• Impaired kidney function can lead to reduced clearance of phosphorus from the blood.\n• Reduced activity of the parathyroid glands can disrupt calcium and phosphorus balance.\n• Low blood pH can cause phosphate levels to rise.\n• Uncontrolled diabetes, certain bone diseases.\nLow Phosphorus Levels\n• Malnutrition or Malabsorption\n• Alcohol can interfere with phosphorus absorption.\n• Overactivity of the parathyroid glands can lead to decreased phosphorus levels.\nAssociated Test: Calcium, Magnesium, PTH, Vitamin D, RFT, Alkaline phosphatase, Electrolytes,24-hour urine phosphate.\nReference:\n1. Henry's Clinical Diagnosis and Management by Laboratory Methods 24th Edition, Page no. 210-211\n2. The Harriet Lane Handbook 23rd Edition, Page no. 740-741.\n3. Kit insert\n----- End of Report -----\nPatient Registered on :09-03-2026, 11:12 AM\nSample Collected on : 09-03-2026, 11:12 AM\nTest Released on : 09-03-2026, 11:20 AM\nSample ID : 69AE5DDD0EAFC\nSample Type : Serum Dr. Hemawati Dhritlahare\nMD (Medicine, Gold Medalist), PhD (Cardiology)\nPage : 4 / 5 Powered By :\n\n-- 4 of 5 --\n\nPatient ID :\nPatient Name : Mr. Ravi Kumar\nAge / Gender : 55 Years / Male\nOrder Status : Final\nRef. By : Self\nReport Date : 09-03-2026\nSample Collected at : Pt. J.N.M Medical college\nMedical College, Raipur, Raipur, Chhattisgarh, 490001\nSample Processed at : Dhritlahare Diagnostics & Pathology\n, Raipur, Raipur, Chhattisgarh, 492001\nClinical Chemistry\nInvestigation Results Unit Bio. Ref. Inter-\nval\nMethod\nRenal Function test (RFT / KFT), Serum\nCreatinine 8.0 mg/dl 0.9 - 1.3 Jaffes Kinetic\nUrea Serum 158 mg/dl 10 - 40 Urease UV\nSodium (Na) 136 mmol/L 136 - 142 ISE\nPotassium (K) 5.9 mmol/L 3.8 - 5.0 ISE\nEstimated Glomerular Filtration Rate\n(eGFR)\n48 mL/min/{1.73_m2}>=90 Calculated\n>= 90, (G1)– Normal kidney function if no other evidence of kidney disease> 60–89, (G2) – May be normal unless other signs of kidney damage\nare present> 45–59, (G3a) – Kidney damage likely; monitor and manage risk factors> 30–44, (G3b) – Moderate kidney disease; increased risk of\ncomplications> 15–29, (G4) – Severe kidney damage; preparation for renal replacement therapy> Less than15 (G5) – Kidney failure (ESRD), Kidney\nfailure; dialysis or transplant usually required\nRFT/KFT Interpretation\nParameter Low Value Interpretation High Value Interpretation\nBlood Urea - Liver dysfunction, Malnutrition> - Kidney dysfunction, Dehydration, GI bleeding>\nSerum Creatinine - Muscle wasting, Low protein diet> - AKD/CKD, Rhabdomyolysis>\nUric Acid - Wilson's disease, SIADH, Fanconi syndrome> - Gout, Renal dysfunction, Leukemia/Cancer>\nSodium - SIADH, Addison's disease, heart failure - Dehydration, diabetes insipidus\nPotassium - Diuretics, vomiting, diarrhea - Renal failure, acidosis, drugs (ACEi)\nChloride - Vomiting, metabolic alkalosis - Dehydration, renal tubular acidosis\nAlbumin - Nephrotic syndrome, Liver disease, Malnutrition\n>\n- Usually not high unless due to dehydration\nAssociated Test: Calcium, Phosphorus, Vitamin D(25-OH),CBC,HbA1c,Lipid profile, Blood glucose.\nReference:\n1. Henry's clinical diagnosis and management by laboratory method, 24th Edition, page no. 1623.e19, 1623.e20\n2. The Harriet Lane Handbook 23rd Edition, Page no. 738, 741, 742\n3. Kit Insert\n----- End of Report -----\nIMPORTANT INSTRUCTIONS\nTest results depend on the specimen quality and need clinical correlation by the referring physician. Delays in report\ndelivery may occur; apologies for any inconvenience. Additional analyses at extra cost require prompt follow-up.\nResults may vary between labs and aren't valid for Medico legal purposes. Reports are computer-validated and don't\nneed a physical signature. Contact the lab for unexpected results.\nPatient Registered on :09-03-2026, 11:12 AM\nSample Collected on : 09-03-2026, 11:12 AM\nTest Released on : 09-03-2026, 11:20 AM\nSample ID : 69AE5DDD0EAFC\nSample Type : Serum Dr. Hemawati Dhritlahare\nMD (Medicine, Gold Medalist), PhD (Cardiology)\nPage : 5 / 5 Powered By :\n\n-- 5 of 5 --\n\n","analysis":"Okay, I will analyze the report and generate the requested information according to all instructions.\n\nHere's the formatted response containing the lab values, reference ranges, classifications, explanations, associated risks, and the specified sections:\n\n| Parameter | Value (with Unit) | Reference Range (with Unit) | Status | Explanation | Risks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Calcium Total | 6.4 mg/dl | 8.4-10.2 Enzymatic | Low | Your calcium level of 6.4 mg/dl is considered low. This could potentially be related to hypoparathyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, or another underlying condition affecting calcium regulation.| N/A |\n| Phosphorus | 5 mg/dL | 2.8 - 4.5 UV | High | Your phosphorus level of 5 mg/dL is higher than the typical reference range. It might suggest impaired kidney function, malnutrition, or other conditions impacting phosphorus metabolism. | N/A |\n| Creatinine | 8.0 mg/dl | 0.9 - 1.3 Jaffes | High | Your creatinine level of 8.0 mg/dl is significantly elevated, suggesting impaired kidney function. This requires further investigation to identify the potential cause.| N/A |\n| Urea Serum | 158 mg/dl | 10 - 40 Urease | High | Your urea serum level of 158 mg/dl is considerably high, pointing towards possible kidney problems or poor nutritional status. Further testing would be needed to confirm the cause.| N/A |\n| Potassium (K) | 5.9 mmol/L | 3.8 - 5.0 ISE | High | Your potassium level of 5.9 mmol/L is slightly elevated. While generally benign, persistently elevated potassium can sometimes signal kidney impairment or hormonal imbalances. | N/A |\n| Sodium (Na) | 136 mmol/L | 136-142 ISE | Normal | Your sodium level of 136 mmol/L is within the normal range. | N/A |\n| Estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR)| 48 mL/min/{1.73_m2}| >=90 Calculated | Low | Your estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) of 48 mL/min/{1.73_m2} is below optimal. This indicates mild to moderate reduction in kidney function. | N/A |\n\nReason Specified\n- Calcium Total (Low): Possible hypoparathyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, or other conditions affecting calcium regulation.\n- Phosphorus (High): Impaired kidney function, malnutrition, or other conditions impacting phosphorus metabolism.\n- Creatinine (High): Impaired kidney function. Requires further investigation to identify the potential cause.\n- Urea Serum (High): Possible kidney problems or poor nutritional status. Further testing would be needed to confirm the cause.\n- Potassium (K) (High): Slightly elevated; while generally benign, persistently elevated potassium can sometimes signal kidney impairment or hormonal imbalances.\n- Sodium (Na) (Normal): Within the normal range.\n- Estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR)(Low): Mild to moderate reduction in kidney function. Indicates mild to moderate reduction in kidney function.\n\nSignature Verification\nNo authorized physician's signature was found on this report. The report may not have been reviewed by a qualified healthcare professional.\n\nOverall Assessment & Recommendations\n- Dietary Modification: Focus on reducing processed foods, limiting excessive salt intake, and ensuring sufficient hydration.\n- Specialist Referral: Consider referral to nephrology for further assessment and guidance regarding managing your compromised kidney function.\n- Follow Up: Recheck eGFR and creatinine in 4-6 weeks after making necessary lifestyle modifications and discussing treatment options with your nephrologist. Monitor fluid intake closely.\n\n## Probable Causes\n- Calcium Total (Low): Possible hypoparathyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, or other conditions affecting calcium regulation.\n- Phosphorus (High): Impaired kidney function, malnutrition, or other conditions impacting phosphorus metabolism.\n- Creatinine (High): Impaired kidney function. Requires further investigation to identify the potential cause.\n- Urea Serum (High): Possible kidney problems or poor nutritional status. Further testing would be needed to confirm the cause.\n- Potassium (K) (High): Persistently elevated potassium can sometimes signal kidney impairment or hormonal imbalances.\n\n## Precautions\n- Avoid medications known to affect kidney function, especially NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors, without prior discussion with your doctor.\n- Maintain good oral hygiene to prevent urinary tract infections, which can worsen kidney function.\n- Stay well-hydrated throughout the day.\n\n## Immediate Actions\n- Consult a General Physician for a comprehensive evaluation of your abnormal lab values.\n\n## When to Worry\n- Seek immediate medical attention if you develop swelling in your legs or ankles, sudden weight gain, nausea, vomiting, confusion, or difficulty breathing. These symptoms may indicate severe complications of kidney dysfunction.\n\n## Recommended Doctor Consultation\n- General Physician\n- Nephrologist\n\n## Follow Up\nAll your report values are within normal range. Recommended follow-up: Repeat these tests after 6 months as part of routine health monitoring. Maintain a balanced diet, stay adequately hydrated, and continue taking prescribed medications regularly.","riskLevel":"critical","parameters":[{"name":"Calcium Total","unit":"mg/dl","value":6.4,"status":"Low","rangeLow":8.4,"rawRange":"8.4-10.2 Enzymatic","rangeHigh":10.2},{"name":"Phosphorus","unit":"mg/dL","value":5,"status":"High","rangeLow":2.8,"rawRange":"2.8-4.5 UV","rangeHigh":4.5},{"name":"Creatinine","unit":"mg/dl","value":8,"status":"High","rangeLow":0.9,"rawRange":"0.9-1.3 Jaffes","rangeHigh":1.3},{"name":"Urea Serum","unit":"mg/dl","value":158,"status":"High","rangeLow":10,"rawRange":"10-40 Urease","rangeHigh":40},{"name":"Potassium (K)","unit":"mmol/L","value":5.9,"status":"High","rangeLow":3.8,"rawRange":"3.8-5.0 ISE","rangeHigh":5},{"name":"Sodium (Na)","unit":"mmol/L","value":136,"status":"Normal","rangeLow":136,"rawRange":"136-142 ISE","rangeHigh":142},{"name":"Estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR)","unit":"mL/min/{1.73_m2}","value":48,"status":"Low","rangeLow":90,"rawRange":">=90 Calculated","rangeHigh":99999}],"createdAt":"2026-03-17T17:41:19.436Z"}]
