6:39:57 AM [express] POST /api/ai/chat 200 in 101858ms :: {"response":"Alright, let's create a comprehensive weight loss diet plan for Mani. Given his information, my focus will be on creating a calorie deficit while ensuring adequate nutritional balance and satiety. Keep in mind that individual results may vary. Regular monitoring of progress and adjusting the plan as needed is vital. Also remember to consult with a Registered Dietitian or Healthcare Professional for personalized recommendations.\n\n**Key Principles:**\n\n*   **Calorie Deficit:** Aim for a controlled calorie reduction of roughly 500-750 calories per day relative to his maintenance requirements. Tracking food intake diligently is critical for success. Utilize online tools or apps like MyFitnessPal or Cronometer.\n*   **Focus on Nutrient Density:** Prioritize foods packed with vitamins, minerals, and fiber despite their lower calorie count. Examples include fruits, vegetables, lean proteins, and whole grains.\n*   **Adequate Protein Intake:** Maintaining sufficient protein consumption (~0.8-1 gram per pound of body weight) is important for preserving muscle mass during weight loss and boosting fullness.\n*   **Limit Processed Foods, Sugars, and Excessive Fats:** Minimize intake of highly processed items, sugary drinks, unhealthy saturated and trans fats, and excessive alcohol.\n*   **Stay Hydrated:** Drinking ample water (at least eight glasses daily) assists with appetite suppression, metabolic rate enhancement, and waste removal.\n*   **Consistent Activity Level**: Incorporate both cardiovascular exercise (cardio) and strength training into weekly routine. Cardio burns calories whereas strength training builds muscle which increases resting metabolic rate.\n\n**Meal Plan Example:**\n\n**Breakfast (Approximately 300-350 Calories):**\n\n*   Scrambled Eggs (2 whole eggs + 2 egg whites) with Spinach and Tomatoes sauteed in minimal olive oil. Served with 1 slice of whole-wheat toast.\n    *   Rationale: Egg white provides protein with very little fat. Leafy Greens contain Vitamins and Minerals whilst remaining low calorie. Toast acts as a filler item.\n\n**Mid-Morning Snack (Approximately 150-200 Calories):**\n\n*   Plain Greek Yogurt (6 oz) with 1/4 cup Blueberries. Sprinkle of Chia Seeds optional.\n    *   Rationale: Greek yogurt is rich in protein and keeps you feeling fuller longer. Blueberries deliver antioxidants and fiber. Chia seeds increase satiety factor.\n\n**Lunch (Approximately 400-450 Calories):**\n\n*   Grilled Chicken Salad Wrap: Large Lettuce Leaves Filled with 4 oz Grilled Chicken Breast, Chopped Cucumbers, Bell Peppers, Shredded Carrot, Light Vinaigrette Dressing.\n    *   Rationale: Lean protein satisfies hunger. Vegetables provide bulk and nutrients. Choosing a wrap allows for better portion control versus eating a sandwich.\n\n**Afternoon Snack (Approximately 150-200 Calories):**\n\n*   Apple Sliced With two Tablespoons of Natural Almond Butter.\n    *   Rationale: Apples provide Fiber and Vitamin C. Nuts provide healthy fats and protein.\n\n**Dinner (Approximately 500-600 Calories):**\n\n*   Baked Cod (4oz) with Roasted Brussel Sprouts and Garlic Herb Infused Steamers.\n    *   Rationale: Low calory fish with loads of vitamin B12. Brussell Sprouts are great source of Fibre.\n\n**Foods to Avoid/Restrict Heavily:**\n\n*   Sugary Drinks (Soda, Juice, Energy Drinks)\n*   Processed Snacks (Chips, Cookies, Candy)\n*   Fried Foods\n*   Refined Grains (White Bread, White Pasta, Cereals High in Sugar)\n*   Excessively Fatty Cuts of Meat\n*   Cream Sauces and Rich Dressings\n*   Frozen Meals and Prepackaged Convenience Items\n\n**Reasoning Behind the Plan:**\n\n*   The breakfast focuses on protein","followUpQuestions":[]}
