6:44:58 AM [express] POST /api/ai/chat 200 in 103325ms :: {"response":"Okay, here's a sample Indian diet plan geared towards weight loss. Please keep in mind that everyone responds differently to diets, so listen carefully to your own body and make adjustments as needed. **Always consult with a doctor or registered dietitian before beginning any new diet.** Portion control remains key regardless of whether you eat traditional Indian cuisine.\n\n**Core Principles:**\n\n*   **Reduce Oil Consumption:** Opt for healthier cooking methods like steaming, baking, grilling, or shallow frying with minimal oil.\n*   **Increase Vegetable Intake:** Load up on salads, vegetable curries, and raitas. Include a wide array of colors for maximum nutrient benefit.\n*   **Choose Whole Grains Over Refined:** Replace white rice with brown rice, roti with whole wheat chapati, and polished dals with unpolished ones where possible.\n*   **Control Portions:** Even healthy foods become problematic when eaten excessively. Practice mindful eating and pay attention to your stomach signals.\n*   **Include Legumes Regularly:** They are excellent sources of protein and fiber, helping you feel satisfied and regulate blood sugar.\n*   **Spice Up Your Food:** Spices enhance flavors and can potentially aid in weight management. Turmeric, ginger, cumin, coriander, etc.\n*   **Drink Plenty of Water:** Stay hydrated throughout the day.\n\n**Example Daily Meal Plan:**\n\n**Breakfast (Around 300-350 Calories):**\n\n*   **Option 1:** Besan Cheela (made with besan flour, onion, and spices) with a dollop of curd (yogurt) and a side of spiced buttermilk (chaas).\n*   **Option 2:** Moong Dal Khichdi (lentils cooked slowly with rice) flavored with turmeric and mild spices.  Serve with a squeeze of lime.\n*   **Avoid:** Excess ghee, heavy cream, oily toppings.\n\n**Mid-Morning Snack (Around 150-200 Calories):**\n\n*   Roasted Chana Chaat (chickpeas tossed with spices and lemon juice).\n*   Cucumber Raisins Salad with Mint leaves.\n*   A small bowl of sprouted moong dal.\n\n**Lunch (Around 400-450 Calories):**\n\n*   **Option 1:** Rajma Masala (kidney bean curry) with Brown Rice and a simple salad of chopped Cucumber, Tomato, Onion, and Cilantro.\n*   **Option 2:** Palak Paneer (Spinach and Cottage Cheese Curry) with Multigrain Chapati (whole wheat flatbread) and a side of Saag Muthiya (green gourd dumplings).\n*   **Avoid:** Creamy sauces, deep-fried accompaniments, overly rich gravies.\n\n**Evening Snack (Around 100-150 Calories):**\n\n*   Sprouted Makhana (foxnuts) sprinkled with chaat masala.\n*   A small apple with a teaspoon of honey.\n*   Green tea with a pinch of ginger.\n\n**Dinner (Around 500-600 Calories):**\n\n*   **Option 1:** Chicken Tikka (marinated and grilled chicken pieces) with a generous amount of raw salad (cucumber, tomato, onion, cilantro).\n*   **Option 2:** Vegetarian Thali featuring various dishes including Sabzi (vegetable dish), Daal (lentil stew), Jeera Rice, and Raita (Yogurt dip). Choose lighter options within the thali.\n*   **Avoid:** Heavy gravies, fried appetizers, desserts laden with sugar.\n\n**Foods to Reduce or Eliminate:**\n\n*   Deep Fried Dishes (Pakoras, samosas, jalebi)\n*   Sweet Meats (Gulab Jamun, Rasmalai, Barfi)\n*   Rich Dairy Products (Full-Fat Curd","followUpQuestions":[]}
