7:11:49 AM [express] POST /api/ai/chat 200 in 84195ms :: {"response":"Okay, here's an Indian Diet Plan tailored for Mani (age 22), aiming for healthy weight loss while considering his blood type and other factors. Remember that this is general advice; it’s crucial he consults with a registered dietitian or nutritionist for truly individualized guidance based on specific needs and health conditions. Also note I am not able to provide any medical diagnosis/treatment as per my limitations.\n\n**Important Considerations:**\n\n*   **Calorie Deficit:** To lose weight effectively, Mani will need to consume fewer calories than he burns. A deficit of around 500 calories below maintenance level should result in approximately one pound lost per week. We can adjust calorie targets once we have more information about his activity levels.\n*   **Hydration:** Drink plenty of water throughout the day – at least 8 glasses. Water helps boost metabolism, keeps you feeling fuller, and aids digestion.\n*   **Portion Control:** Be mindful of portion sizes! Use smaller plates and bowls if needed. Measure food initially until you get used to appropriate amounts.\n*   **Regular Exercise:** Combining this dietary plan with regular physical activity significantly enhances weight loss results. Aim for at least 30 minutes of moderate exercise most days of the week.\n*  **Blood Type Consideration**: While there isn't strong scientific evidence supporting strict adherence to all aspects of Ayurveda related to blood group diets alone, some people find certain foods easier to digest when following their recommended guidelines. The suggestions made would be generally applicable regardless of blood type but may require adjustments depending upon individual tolerance.\n\n**Indian Diet Plan - Weight Loss Focus**\n\n**Breakfast (Approx. 400 Calories)**\n\n*   Option 1: **Oats Upma:** Prepare upma using oats instead of semolina. Add vegetables like carrots, peas, beans, onions, tomatoes, and green chilies. Season with turmeric powder, cumin seeds, coriander leaves, and black pepper. Serve with a side of lightly spiced yogurt (plain Greek Yogurt) .\n    *   Why? Oats offer fiber which promotes satiety. Vegetables increase nutrient intake without adding excessive calories. Plain yogurt adds protein & probiotics.\n*   Option 2: **Poha (without onion):** Make poha from flattened rice, skipping the onion altogether. Sauté mustard seeds, curry leaves, and hing (asafoetida) first. Then add the poha along with chopped cilantro, lemon juice, and a pinch of salt.\n     *   Why? Poha provides carbohydrates and nutrients. Skipping onion reduces potential bloating issues for some individuals. Lemon Juice offers Vitamin C.\n* Option 3 : **Sprouted Moong Chilla:** Sprout moong dal overnight then mix into a batter with finely grated carrot, spinach and spices such as ginger garlic paste ,turmeric powder etc.. Cook chillas on a non stick pan and serve them with tomato ketchup(low sugar version )or mint chutney.\n        *Why?: It has high amount of proteins and fibres due to sprouting process making u feel satiated longer.\n\n**Mid-Morning Snack (Approx. 150 Calories)**\n\n*   Option 1: **Apple slices with Almond Butter:** One medium apple sliced served with two tablespoons of almond butter.\n    *   Why? Apples contain soluble fiber aiding digestive system function. Almond butter contributes healthy fats and protein providing sustained energy release preventing overeating later.\n*   Option 2: **Roasted Chickpeas:** Roast chickpeas seasoned with herbs and spices (cumin, chili powder, paprika) till crispy.\n    *   Why? Provides plant-based protein and fibre helping curb hunger pangs between meals.\n*   Option 3: **Small bowl of sprouts Salad.** Mix different types of sprouted lentils, radish, cucumber, capsicum and season it with lime juice and chaat masala.\n      *Why?: High in vitamins minerals and fibers keeping body hydrated","followUpQuestions":[]}
